Output/
.gocache/
.vscode/
*.exe

# NOT ignored: cmd/.
#
# It was on this list from 2026-03-25 ("add eth support", which rewrote this
# whole file) until 2026-09-08, sitting among the build and editor artefacts.
# That predates cmd/porttool by about six months, and nothing under cmd/ had
# ever been tracked - so it was never a decision about the port tool, it was a
# stale line the port tool happened to land behind.
#
# What it cost: PortTool's main package was not in version control at all, so a
# fresh clone could not build it - compile_tool.sh would look for a package
# that is not there.
# Machine-local paths. Both files are GENERATED by tools/init_machine.py, which
# also keeps the first version it ever replaced as a .bak. The committed record
# of what belongs in them is the SETTINGS table in that script -- there is no
# template to copy, because a template would be a second place to keep the same
# list and the two would drift.
#
# Both languages are listed while M7 (the PowerShell -> Python rewrite) is in
# progress; machine.ps1 stops being generated at step 6.
TestCase/config/machine.ps1
TestCase/config/machine.py
TestCase/config/*.bak
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# .claude/settings.json IS committed -- it is the shared, machine-independent
# permission allowlist. Only the local override is private. Listing it here
# rather than relying on a global gitignore, which a new machine will not have.
# The trailing * also covers the .bak that init_machine.py keeps of the
# first version it ever replaced.
.claude/settings.local.json*

# Reports a plan run leaves behind. The sink in a plan file is relative to the
# plan, so a run drops these next to it - correct on a production PC, noise in
# a checkout. 55 of them had piled up by 2026-09-08.
TestCase/plans/reports/
TestCase/host/porttool_plan/reports/
